{{also|FET}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/fɛt/|lang=en}}

===Etymology 1===
{{PIE root|en|ped}}
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{m|enm|fetten}}, {{m|enm|feten}}, from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{m|ang|fetian}}, {{m|ang|fatian||to bring, fetch}}, probably a conflation of {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*fetaną||to go}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{m|ine-pro|*ped-||to walk, stumble, fall}}; and {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*fatōną||to hold, seize}}, also from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{m|ine-pro|*ped-}}. Cognate with Dutch {{m|nl|vatten||to catch, grab}}, German {{m|de|fassen||to lay hold of, seize, take, hold}}. Compare also Icelandic {{m|is|feta||to find one's way}}. More at {{m|en|fetch}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb|fets|fetting|fet}}

# {{label|en|obsolete}} To [[fetch]].

===Etymology 2===
Compare {{m|en|feat}}, {{etyl|fr|-}} {{m|fr|fait}}, and {{etyl|it|-}} {{m|it|fetta||slice}}, {{etyl|de|-}} {{m|de|Fetzen||rag}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{label|en|obsolete}} A [[piece]].
#* Michael Drayton
#*: The bottom clear,<br>Now laid with many a '''fet'''<br>Of seed pearl.

{{Webster 1913}}

===Anagrams===
* {{l|en|eft}}, {{l|en|EFT}}

